# db/seed.sql is generated by src/scripts/db.py, which writes LF on purpose
# (_dump opens the temp file with newline="\n"). With core.autocrlf=true and no
# rule here, git checks the file out as CRLF on Windows, so every pipeline run
# leaves it reported as modified while `git diff` shows nothing at all.
#
# That noise is not cosmetic. The committed dump is how two machines stay in
# sync, and its entire premise -- stated in CLAUDE.md -- is that a conflict or a
# change here means somebody changed data. A file that looks modified after a
# no-op run erodes exactly that signal, and makes a real change easy to miss.
db/seed.sql text eol=lf
